1
00:00:00,250 --> 00:00:01,533
Every backlog drifts.

2
00:00:01,533 --> 00:00:04,432
Stories without acceptance criteria.

3
00:00:04,432 --> 00:00:06,720
Features without a parent.

4
00:00:06,720 --> 00:00:08,291
Sizes nobody agreed.

5
00:00:08,291 --> 00:00:11,190
Halyard is the product owner's mate.

6
00:00:11,190 --> 00:00:17,510
It reads the backlog, finds the drift, drafts the fix, and waits for a person to approve it.

7
00:00:17,510 --> 00:00:19,404
Nothing moves without your say.

8
00:00:19,654 --> 00:00:22,053
Here is how Halyard fits together.

9
00:00:22,053 --> 00:00:24,308
Everything runs on one machine.

10
00:00:24,308 --> 00:00:29,060
A PowerShell launcher compiles the engine in memory, and the browser talks to it on localhost.

11
00:00:29,760 --> 00:00:36,368
Inside the engine: review, intelligence for drafting and sizing, planning, and a proposal store.

12
00:00:36,368 --> 00:00:38,080
Every change starts as a proposal.

13
00:00:38,780 --> 00:00:42,660
Halyard reads your backlog from GitHub, read only.

14
00:00:42,660 --> 00:00:46,979
The write gateway lets it touch exactly one place, the backlog repository.

15
00:00:47,679 --> 00:00:51,073
Model calls go through Fairlead and nowhere else.

16
00:00:51,073 --> 00:00:54,722
Halyard's own key sits in Windows Credential Manager.

17
00:00:54,722 --> 00:00:58,074
The OpenAI key stays in Lazarette, shared to Fairlead.

18
00:00:58,575 --> 00:01:04,106
Out the other side: four tables for Semaphore, and the weekly dashboard as an Outlook draft.

19
00:01:04,806 --> 00:01:09,697
Dashed means planned: live Shellback skills, and a runner on your repositories.

20
00:01:10,397 --> 00:01:11,675
One rule holds it together.

21
00:01:12,375 --> 00:01:14,356
Nothing is written until a person approves.

22
00:01:15,106 --> 00:01:18,195
Every tool in the fleet needs a model key.

23
00:01:18,195 --> 00:01:20,786
At OpenAI, create a new secret key.

24
00:01:21,544 --> 00:01:23,801
Name it something generic.

25
00:01:23,801 --> 00:01:25,668
I call mine fleet-tools.

26
00:01:25,668 --> 00:01:27,626
Seven days is plenty.

27
00:01:28,450 --> 00:01:29,447
Copy it once.

28
00:01:30,006 --> 00:01:31,574
OpenAI never shows it again.

29
00:01:31,824 --> 00:01:36,227
The key goes into Lazarette, the encrypted vault on this machine.

30
00:01:36,927 --> 00:01:40,081
Test credential checks it against OpenAI's read-only endpoint.

31
00:01:40,668 --> 00:01:42,006
HTTP two hundred.

32
00:01:42,781 --> 00:01:43,581
The key is good.

33
00:01:44,148 --> 00:01:48,584
Share it with Fairlead by name, and Fairlead sees only what you share.

34
00:01:49,284 --> 00:01:52,463
Fairlead reads it from the vault and connects.

35
00:01:52,463 --> 00:01:54,837
Eighty-three models, in under a second.

36
00:01:55,087 --> 00:01:57,141
Fairlead gives each tool its own key.

37
00:01:57,841 --> 00:01:59,216
Pick Halyard and install it.

38
00:01:59,916 --> 00:02:01,919
It lands in Windows Credential Manager.

39
00:02:02,619 --> 00:02:08,304
Halyard's config file names the backlog it reads, the backlog it may write to, and the write mode.

40
00:02:08,304 --> 00:02:10,752
The browser can't change any of it.

41
00:02:11,452 --> 00:02:12,971
Test the model.

42
00:02:12,971 --> 00:02:15,364
Under two seconds, end to end.

43
00:02:16,114 --> 00:02:19,934
For this walkthrough we seed a synthetic backlog.

44
00:02:19,934 --> 00:02:23,304
Thirty items, a quarter of them with deliberate gaps.

45
00:02:24,004 --> 00:02:29,317
Each one is a real GitHub issue with a run label, so the whole run comes out again in one click.

46
00:02:29,317 --> 00:02:32,924
It takes about two minutes, paced for the rate limits.

47
00:02:33,624 --> 00:02:35,592
Thirty of thirty.

48
00:02:35,592 --> 00:02:37,783
The backlog is live.

49
00:02:38,163 --> 00:02:45,943
The overview shows the shape of the backlog and what needs attention: items to review, missing parents, topology errors.

50
00:02:45,943 --> 00:02:50,554
Scroll down for the findings by severity, and the next actions.

51
00:02:51,254 --> 00:02:55,368
Review checks every item against its level's definition of ready and the parent chain.

52
00:02:55,949 --> 00:02:58,135
Each finding names the fix that closes it.

53
00:02:58,835 --> 00:03:01,742
Hierarchy shows the tree, theme to task.

54
00:03:01,992 --> 00:03:06,012
Draft missing sections sends each gap to the model through Fairlead.

55
00:03:06,012 --> 00:03:08,485
Real model, a few seconds per item.

56
00:03:09,185 --> 00:03:11,152
Six drafts, all high confidence.

57
00:03:11,152 --> 00:03:13,551
Each is a proposal with a reason.

58
00:03:13,551 --> 00:03:19,221
Open one and read the diff: the model adds what's missing and leaves the rest alone.

59
00:03:19,221 --> 00:03:23,590
And if it ever drops a section, Halyard puts it back word for word.

60
00:03:24,290 --> 00:03:27,796
Approve the high-confidence drafts, then apply.

61
00:03:27,796 --> 00:03:31,629
Six written to the backlog on GitHub, and the apply is recorded.

62
00:03:31,629 --> 00:03:34,653
Every apply is recorded with what it replaced.

63
00:03:35,209 --> 00:03:38,150
Undo previews first, then restores the earlier text.

64
00:03:38,471 --> 00:03:39,602
Six items restored.

65
00:03:40,302 --> 00:03:43,407
Anything a person edited since is left alone.

66
00:03:43,407 --> 00:03:48,382
The proposals go back to pending, so approve and apply again when you're ready.

67
00:03:48,632 --> 00:03:50,548
Work comes in from anywhere.

68
00:03:50,548 --> 00:03:54,469
A raw ticket, or a note typed straight into Intake.

69
00:03:54,469 --> 00:03:56,783
Two loose notes, no structure at all.

70
00:03:57,395 --> 00:03:58,287
Preview the drafts.

71
00:03:58,287 --> 00:04:05,394
Halyard turns each note into a structured work item and copies it into the backlog, with a link back to where it started.

72
00:04:05,773 --> 00:04:08,102
Plan fills the next sprints from ready stories.

73
00:04:08,802 --> 00:04:10,620
Capacity comes from the team file.

74
00:04:10,620 --> 00:04:15,888
Owners are matched on skills, and anything that slipped last sprint goes first.

75
00:04:16,588 --> 00:04:18,930
Skills come from Shellback peer cards today.

76
00:04:19,615 --> 00:04:21,236
A live Shellback link is planned.

77
00:04:21,736 --> 00:04:25,210
Upward, Halyard drafts the portfolio story for leadership.

78
00:04:25,210 --> 00:04:27,379
This one is the live model, thinking.

79
00:04:28,079 --> 00:04:33,654
Themes, priorities, risks and the next quarter, with the facts every claim rests on.

80
00:04:33,654 --> 00:04:37,250
Everything it says about the backlog, it can point to.

81
00:04:37,992 --> 00:04:47,167
The leadership page puts features on track, velocity, ready backlog, capacity and blockers on one screen.

82
00:04:47,167 --> 00:04:51,011
One click exports backlog health for Semaphore, as four tables ready to chart.

83
00:04:51,606 --> 00:04:56,439
The weekly dashboard opens in Outlook as a draft, so a person reviews it and presses send.

84
00:04:57,124 --> 00:05:01,375
Next on the chart: a runner on the team's repositories.

85
00:05:01,375 --> 00:05:05,984
It watches commits and pull requests, and writes the story for the work it sees.

86
00:05:06,483 --> 00:05:08,465
Developers stop doing the admin.

87
00:05:08,465 --> 00:05:16,064
The product owner approves what the runner proposes, through the same diff and the same gate.

88
00:05:16,064 --> 00:05:17,001
Production stays shut.

89
00:05:17,001 --> 00:05:24,782
Promote needs a production write mode, an explicit allow flag, a typed phrase and a diff, and none of it can be switched on from the browser.

90
00:05:25,482 --> 00:05:28,588
The write audit lists every write Halyard made.

91
00:05:29,115 --> 00:05:30,958
Every one went to the backlog.

92
00:05:30,958 --> 00:05:33,276
Halyard proposes.

93
00:05:33,276 --> 00:05:35,185
People decide.

94
00:05:35,185 --> 00:05:37,503
That's the watch.

95
00:05:37,503 --> 00:05:39,004
Fair winds.
